      ":question/id": "556446bf-a987-5d2f-81ee-912d5d6f9e07",
      ":question/problem": "![](generate/scratch/17592186179742/assets/5d5bc777cb3d8c8a736c-prompt.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the elliptic arc is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion.\n\n![](generate/scratch/17592186179742/assets/5d5bc777cb3d8c8a736c-forward.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ the force is still perpendicular to the motion.\n\n![](generate/scratch/17592186179742/assets/5d5bc777cb3d8c8a736c-reverse.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
